  "live_records": {
    "q-142449": {
      "dom_id": "step-q142449",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14188481/assets/0b5037831d90f93fb6b8b0603655162e4f362a638b35c569f7c5e07007974b3d.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=16$ from a normal population. Given that the sample mean is $50,$ and the sample standard deviation is $8,$ find a $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>1.753)=0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $15$ degrees of freedom,  as shown in the diagram above.*",
      "worked_solution": "We are told that the population is normally distributed, but we do not know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$\n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nfollows a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=50,\\,n=16,\\,s=8.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>1.753)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}={t}_{15,0.05}=1.753.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 50\\pm 1.753\\cdot \\frac{8}{\\sqrt{16}} \\\\  & 50\\pm 3.506\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
