    "q-142571": {
      "dom_id": "step-q142571",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14188481/assets/4c9b1e09734e74c098fd5e91eb3c72079157c3a69b1bb1d039bb46e823e5b7b6.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=25$ from a normal population. Given that the sample mean is $35.5,$ and the sample standard deviation is $3,$ find a $95%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>2.064)=0.025,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $24$ degrees of freedom,  as shown in the diagram above.*",
      "worked_solution": "We are told that the population is normally distributed, but we do not know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$\n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nfollows a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom. \n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=35.5,\\,n=25,\\,s=3.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>2.064)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}={t}_{24,0.025}=2.064.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $95%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}35.5 & \\pm 2.064\\cdot \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{25}} \\\\ 35.5 & \\pm 1.238\\end{aligned}\n$$",
