    "q-142512": {
      "dom_id": "step-q142512",
      "name": "Question 10",
      "problem": "Consider a sample of size $n=256$ from a population. Given that the sample mean is $105,$ and the sample standard deviation is $36,$ find a $95%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>1.969)=0.025,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $255$ degrees of freedom.*",
      "worked_solution": "We do not know the distribution of the population, nor do we know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ However, the sample size $n=256\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. \n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated by the student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=105,\\,n=256,\\,s=36.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>1.969)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}={t}_{255,0.025}=1.969.\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14188481/assets/d108130264501813e8bfed332f345d8d005095127c5d9d8e169a1c2ae83b2ec7.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the $95%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}105 & \\pm 1.969\\cdot \\frac{36}{\\sqrt{256}} \\\\ 105 & \\pm 4.430\\end{aligned}\n$$",
